# Sealed Results & Errors per Layer

Every layer of the hexagon (incoming ports/use cases, outgoing ports, adapters) models its outcomes with **two distinct sealed interfaces**:

1. **`Result`** — expected business outcomes, **including business errors** (not-found, duplicate, validation rejection). These travel as **values** in the `Uni` item. They are part of the normal flow and must **not** abort transactions.
2. **`Error`** — exceptional failures (infrastructure broken, invariant violated, corrupt state) that must **fail the `Uni`** as an **exception**, so Mutiny and `@Transactional`/reactive transaction handling can intercept them and **roll back**.

## Result — sealed interface of expected outcomes

Declared nested in the port interface, returned as the `Uni` item:

```java
public interface ProcessTransactionCreatedUseCase {

    Uni<Result> execute(Command command);

    sealed interface Result {
        record Success(Position position) implements Result {}
        record DuplicateTransaction(UUID transactionId) implements Result {}   // business error, still a Result
        record Ignored(String reason) implements Result {}
    }

    record Command(UUID transactionId, String ticker, BigDecimal quantity) {}
}
```

- Business rejections are `Result` variants, never exceptions — throwing them would incorrectly roll back work that should be committed (e.g. recording that an event was ignored).
- Consume results with an exhaustive `switch` in `onItem().transform(...)`:

```java
useCase.execute(command)
    .onItem().transform(result -> switch (result) {
        case Result.Success success -> Response.ok(mapper.toDto(success.position())).build();
        case Result.DuplicateTransaction dup -> Response.status(Response.Status.CONFLICT).build();
        case Result.Ignored ignored -> Response.noContent().build();
    });
```

## Error — sealed exception hierarchy for rollback-worthy failures

Each layer defines its errors as a **sealed hierarchy extending `RuntimeException`**, so they propagate as `Uni` failures that transaction management intercepts and rolls back:

```java
public sealed interface PositionPersistenceError {

    sealed abstract class PersistenceException extends RuntimeException implements PositionPersistenceError {
        protected PersistenceException(String message, Throwable cause) { super(message, cause); }
    }

    final class ConnectionFailure extends PersistenceException {
        public ConnectionFailure(Throwable cause) { super("Database unreachable", cause); }
    }

    final class OptimisticLockConflict extends PersistenceException {
        public OptimisticLockConflict(String message) { super(message, null); }
    }
}
```

- These flow through Mutiny's failure channel — never wrap them into a `Result`. Inside a transactional pipeline, a failed `Uni` triggers rollback automatically.
- Because the hierarchy is sealed, `.onFailure(Type.class)` and `switch` over failures stay exhaustive and translation between layers is explicit:

```java
repository.save(position)
    .onFailure(PositionPersistenceError.OptimisticLockConflict.class)
    .transform(failure -> new UseCaseError.ConcurrentModification(command.transactionId(), failure));
```

## Boundary Rules

- Adapters translate raw infrastructure exceptions (SQL, HTTP, Redis, SQS) into the port's sealed `Error` types; raw driver exceptions never cross a port boundary.
- Each layer catches the lower layer's `Error` types and either translates them into its own `Error` (rollback continues to propagate) or — when the failure is actually an expected business case at this level — converts them into a `Result` variant.
- The outermost incoming adapter (controller, consumer) maps `Result` variants to responses/acks and `Error` failures to 5xx responses / retries / DLQ, after the rollback has already happened.
- No fallthrough `default` branches when switching over sealed `Result` or `Error` types — let the compiler enforce exhaustiveness when new variants are added.
